We can write $i$ raised to an integer exponent as one of $1,-1,i,-i$ depending on the remainder when divided by 4.
Thus, $i^x+i^y$ will be real if and only if it is equal to $i+j$ for some $x$ when $x$ has a remainder of 1 when divided by 4 and $j=-1$ when $y$ has a remainder of 3 when divided by 4, or some $i+j$ for some $y$ when $y$ has a remainder of 1 when divided by 4 and $j=-1$ when $x$ has a remainder of 3 when divided by 4.
By counting cases, we get that there are $\lfloor \frac{99}{4} \rfloor = 24$ pairs with $x$ having a remainder of 1 and $y$ having a remainder of 3 or vice versa, and we simply have the number of pairs with $x$ and $y$ having a remainder of 1 or 3. It is rather easy to count, at least by casework. We get
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}\hline $x$ & $y$\\\hline $1$ & $5$ \\ $5$ & $9$ \\ $9$ & $13$ \\ $13$ & $17$ \\ $17$ & $21$ \\ $21$ & $25$ \\ $25$ & $29$ \\ $29$ & $33$ \\ $33$ & $37$ \\ $37$ & $41$ \\ $41$ & $45$ \\ $45$ & $49$ \\ $49$ & $53$ \\ $53$ & $57$ \\ $57$ & $61$ \\ $61$ & $65$ \\ $65$ & $69$ \\ $69$ & $73$ \\ $73$ & $77$ \\ $77$ & $81$ \\ $81$ & $85$ \\ $85$ & $89$ \\ $89$ & $93$ \\ $93$ & $97$ \\ $97$ & $101$ \\ $101$ & $105$ \\\\ \hline\end{tabular}
Continuing like this, we get $24\times 2=48$ pairs with $x$ and $y$ having a remainder of 1 or 3.
By inclusion-exclusion, we subtract the number of pairs where both $x$ and $y$ are, say, $1$, which is $1+1=2$, and we add the number of pairs where both $x$ and $y$ are equal to $10$ (which is $0$ in this case), and continue like this. In total, we get $\boxed{\boxed{128}}$. The answer is \boxed{128}
